e line \\(y=b\\).  \nAll these lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n*\\(k=n\\).*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nEquation (1) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)y=i\\,x+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Choose \\(i=a\\) (note that \\(1\\le a\\le n\\)).  \nSubstituting \\(i=a\\) and \\((x,y)=(a,b)\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n(a+1)b=a\\cdot a+1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b=\\frac{a}{a+1}+\\frac{1}{a+1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the equation of \\(\\ell_a\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\), proving that \\(k=n\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering of a triangle by only sunny lines\n\nWe prove by induction the following statement.\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBase case \\(m=1\\): \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). The line \\(y=x+1\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\nInductive step. Assume the lemma holds for some \\(m\\ge 1\\); we construct a family for \\(m+1\\).\n\n1. By the induction hypothesis we have sunny lines \\(\\mathcal L_m=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\}\\) covering the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m^{\\;*}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_{m+1}\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. The points of \\(T_{m+1}\\) not covered by \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) are precisely the points with \\(a+b=m+2\\); denote this set by  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{m+1}= \\{(i,m+2-i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,m+1\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (Geometrically, \\(H_{m+1}\\) is the “hypotenuse’’ of the triangle \\(T_{m+1}\\).)\n\n3. Choose a sunny line that meets **every** point of \\(H_{m+1}\\).  \n   The line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{m+1}:\\; y = \\frac12\\,x + \\frac{m+2}{2}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   has slope \\(\\frac12\\), which is sunny.  \n   Substituting the coordinates of a point \\((i,m+2-i)\\) from (4) into (5) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   m+2-i = \\frac12 i + \\frac{m+2}{2}\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   2(m+2-i)= i + (m+2)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   2m+4-2i = i+m+2\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   i = m+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\ell_{m+1}\\) passes through the *single* point \\((m+2,0)\\), which is not in \\(T_{m+1}\\).  \n   To obtain a line that actually meets points of \\(H_{m+1}\\) we modify the slope slightly.\n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{m+1}:\\; y = \\frac{m}{m+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{m+1}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{m}{m+1}\\) is sunny.  \n   For a point \\((i,m+2-i)\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m}{m+1}\\,i + \\frac{1}{m+1}= \\frac{mi+1}{m+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Equality with the second coordinate \\(m+2-i\\) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   (m+2-i)(m+1)=mi+1\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   (m+2)(m+1)-i(m+1)=mi+1\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   (m+2)(m+1)-1 = i(2m+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(2m+1\\) and \\(m+1\\) are coprime, the above Diophantine equation has a unique solution  \n\n   \\[\n   i = m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\(\\ell_{m+1}\\) passes through the single point \\((m+1,1)\\) of the hypotenuse.  \n   The remaining \\(m\\) points of \\(H_{m+1}\\) are \\((i,m+2-i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\).  \n   Each of these points already belongs to one of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\): indeed, for any \\(i\\le m\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   (i,m+2-i) = (i,(m+1)-i+1) ,\n   \\]\n\n   and the point \\((i,(m+1)-i)\\) lies in \\(T_m^{\\;*}\\); the line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) that covers \\((i,(m+1)-i)\\) also contains \\((i,m+2-i)\\) because the two points have the same abscissa and differ only by one unit in the ordinate, and the slope of that line is not \\(0\\) (otherwise it would b