            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe required points are exactly the points of \\(T_n\\).  \nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence it lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \nAll these lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n*\\(k=n\\).*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nEquation (1) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)y=i\\,x+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Choose \\(i=a\\) (note that \\(1\\le a\\le n\\)).  \nSubstituting \\(i=a\\) and \\((x,y)=(a,b)\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n(a+1)b=a\\cdot a+1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b=\\frac{a}{a+1}+\\frac{1}{a+1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the equation of \\(\\ell_a\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\), proving that \\(k=n\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering of a triangle by only sunny lines\n\nWe prove by induction the following statement.\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBase case \\(m=1\\): \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). The line \\(y=x+1\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\nInductive step. Assume the lemma holds for some \\(m\\ge 1\\); we construct a family for \\(m+1\\).\n\n1. By the induction hypothesis we have sunny lines \\(\\mathcal L_m=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\}\\) covering the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m^{\\;*}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_{m+1}\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. The points of \\(T_{m+1}\\) not covered by \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) are precisely the points with \\(a+b=m+2\\); denote this set by  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{m+1}= \\{(i,m+2-i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,m+1\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (Geometrically, \\(H_{m+1}\\) is the “hypotenuse’’ of the triangle \\(T_{m+1}\\).)\n\n3. Choose a sunny line that meets **every** point of \\(H_{m+1}\\).  \n   The line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{m+1}:\\; y = \\frac12\\,x + \\frac{m+2}{2}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   has slope \\(\\frac12\\), which is sunny.  \n   Substituting the coordinates of a point \\((i,m+2-i)\\) from (4) into (5) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   m+2-i = \\frac12 i + \\frac{m+2}{2}\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   2(m+2-i)= i + (m+2)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   2m+4-2i = i+m+2\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   i = m+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\ell_{m+1}\\) passes through the *single* point \\((m+2,0)\\), which is not in \\(T_{m+1}\\).  \n   To obtain a line that actually meets points of \\(H_{m+1}\\) we modify the slope slightly.\n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{m+1}:\\; y = \\frac{m}{m+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{m+1}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{m}{m+1}\\) is sunny.  \n   For a point \\((i,m+2-i)\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m}{m+1}\\,i + \\frac{1}{m+1}= \\frac{mi+1}{m+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Equality with the second coordinate \\(m+2-i\\) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   (m+2-i)(m+1)=mi+1\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   (m+2)(m+1)-i(m+1)=mi+1\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   (m+2)(m+1)-1 = i(2m+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(2m+1\\) and \\(m+1\\) are coprime, the above Diophantine equation has a unique solution  \n\n   \\[\n   i = m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\(\\ell_{m+1}\\) passes through the single point \\((m+1,1)\\) of the hypotenuse.  \n   The remaining \\(m\\) points of \\(H_{m+1}\\) are \\((i,m+2-i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\).  \n   Each of these points already belongs to one of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\): indeed, for any \\(i\\le m\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   (i,m+2-i) = (i,(m+1)-i+1) ,\n   \\]\n\n   and the point \\((i,(m+1)-i)\\) lies in \\(T_m^{\\;*}\\); the line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) that covers \\((i,(m+1)-i)\\) also contains \\((i,m+2-i)\\) because the two points have the same abscissa and differ only by one unit in the ordinate, and the slope of that line is not \\(0\\) (otherwise it would be horizontal, which is impossible for a sunny line). Hence every point of \\(H_{m+1}\\) is covered either by \\(\\ell_{m+1}\\) or by a line from \\(\\mathcal L_m\\).\n\n4. The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{m+1}= \\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{\\ell_{m+1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers all points of \\(T_{m+1}\\).\n\nThus the lemma holds for \\(m+1\\), completing the induction. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   The points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on any of the lines (7) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, the condition \\(b\\le n-k\\) is equivalent to \\(a+b\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1\\).)\n\n3. **Sunny part.**  \n   By Lemma 1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points of \\(T_k\\). Denote this family by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_k=\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Full family.**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal S_k\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the members of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\)), and its union contains every point of \\(T_n\\) (points with \\(b\\le n-k\\) are on a horizontal line, the others are in \\(T_k\\) and are covered by \\(\\mathcal S_k\\)).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Non‑existence of other values\n\nThe construction above shows that **every** integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable.  \nSince a configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
