an pass through all points of \\(H_{m+1}\\) because those points lie on the line \\(x+y=m+2\\) of slope \\(-1\\), which is prohibited. The proposed line does not intersect any point of \\(H_{m+1}\\).\n\n* **Location:** Lemma 1, after discarding the previous attempt, “Let \\(\\ell_{m+1}:\\; y = \\frac{m}{m+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{m+1}\\). … Consequently \\(\\ell_{m+1}\\) passes through the single point \\((m+1,1)\\) of the hypotenuse.”  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** – Substituting \\(x=m+1\\) yields \\(y=m+\\frac{1}{m+1}\\neq1\\). Thus \\(\\ell_{m+1}\\) does **not** pass through \\((m+1,1)\\); the claim is false.\n\n* **Location:** Lemma 1, “Each of these points already belongs to one of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\): … the line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) that covers \\((i,(m+1)-i)\\) also contains \\((i,m+2-i)\\) because the two points have the same abscissa …”  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** – A sunny line covering \\((i,(m+1)-i)\\) need not be vertical (vertical lines are non‑sunny), but it can have any non‑zero, non‑\\(-1\\) slope. There is no reason such a line must also pass through \\((i,m+2-i)\\); the argument is invalid.\n\n* **Location:** Section 3, step 2, “The points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on any of the lines (7) are exactly  \n  \\[\n  T_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n  \\]”  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** – After covering with the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,n-k\\), the uncovered points are **not** the set \\(T_k\\). For example, with \\(n=5,\\;k=2\\) the uncovered points are \\((1,4),(2,4),(1,5)\\), none of which belong to \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\). The description of the remaining set is incorrect.\n\n* **Location:** Section 3, step 3, “By Lemma 1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points of \\(T_k\\).”  \n  **Issue:** **Justification Gap** – Lemma 1 has not been proved correctly; relying on it provides no valid justification for the existence of the required sunny lines.\n\n* **Location:** Section 4, “Since a configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.”  \n  **Issue:** **Justification Gap** – While the bounds are obvious, the solution does not demonstrate that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable; the earlier flawed constructions leave this claim unsupported.\n\n---\n\n##"
