            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_s\\) are the antidiagonals of the triangular lattice; \\(|D_s|=s-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), hence belongs to \\(\\ell_b\\).  \nAll \\(\\ell_i\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\; y=x-d\\qquad\\text{for }d\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ |d|\\le n-1,\\ d\\equiv n\\pmod 2 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(L_d\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently every \\(L_d\\) is sunny.  \nBecause \\(a-b\\) has the same parity as \\(a+b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(|a-b|\\le n-1\\) and \\(a-b\\equiv n\\pmod 2\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_{a-b}\\).  \n\nThe number of admissible values of \\(d\\) is exactly \\(n\\) (they are \\(-n+1,-n+3,\\dots ,n-1\\)).  \nThus the family \\(\\{L_d\\}_{d}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The cases \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Step A – sunny lines.*  \nChoose the first \\(k\\) lines of the family \\(\\{L_d\\}\\) defined above; denote them\n\\[\nS_1,\\dots ,S_k .\n\\]\nAll \\(S_i\\) are sunny and distinct.\n\n*Step B – points already covered.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nR:=T_n\\setminus\\bigl(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}S_i\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points not yet covered.\n\n*Step C – covering the remainder.*  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) consider the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j .\n\\]\n\nWe claim that the collection \\(\\{H_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-k}\\) covers \\(R\\).\n\nIndeed, fix an antidiagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nEvery sunny line \\(S_i\\) meets \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point, because two distinct points of the same antidiagonal lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is not sunny.  \nConsequently after removing the points of the \\(k\\) sunny lines, at most \\((s-1)-k\\) points of \\(D_s\\) remain.  \nSince \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\((s-1)-k\\le n-k\\) for every \\(s\\).  \nA horizontal line meets each antidiagonal in at most one point, so the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are sufficient to pick one point from each of the remaining \\((s-1)-k\\) positions of \\(D_s\\).  \nThus every point of \\(R\\) lies on some \\(H_j\\).\n\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines together with the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines give a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.  Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Non‑existence of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line \\(M\\).\n\nWithout loss of generality we may suppose that \\(M\\) is horizontal (the cases “vertical’’ and “slope \\(-1\\)’’ are analogous).  \nThus \\(M\\) is of the form \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).\n\n*Intersection with antidiagonals.*  \nFor each antidiagonal \\(D_s\\) the line \\(M\\) meets \\(D_s\\) in exactly one point (the unique point of \\(D_s\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(c\\)).  \nConsequently after deleting the points of \\(M\\) there remain \\(s-2\\) points in \\(D_s\\) for every \\(s\\ge 3\\).\n\n*Restriction on sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line cannot contain two points of the same antidiagonal, because any two such points lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is non‑sunny.  \nHence each antidiagonal \\(D_s\\) requires **at least** \\(s-2\\) distinct sunny lines to cover its remaining points.\n\nConsider the largest antidiagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\); it contains \\(n\\) points, one of which lies on \\(M\\).  \nThus \\(D_{n+1}\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nNow look at the next antidiagonal \\(D_{n}\\); it also needs at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and at least one of these lines must be different from those already used for \\(D_{n+1}\\) (otherwise a single sunny line would contain two points of \\(D_{n+1}\\), contradicting the previous paragraph).  \n\nProceeding inductively downwards, we see that to cover all antidiagonals we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl[(n-1)+(n-2)+\\dots +1\\bigr]=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\ndistinct sunny lines.  Since the configuration contains only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, this is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for all integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\},\n\\]\n\nand we have proved that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
