The same bound holds for \\(|C|\\).  \n   The product \\(|R|\\cdot|C|\\) is therefore at least \\(\\bigl(\\frac{2n+k}{3}\\bigr)^2\\).  \n   Removing the at most \\(|D|\\le n-k\\) points that lie on selected diagonals leaves at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl(\\frac{2n+k}{3}\\Bigr)^2-(n-k) \\;>\\; k\n   \\]\n\n   for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence \\(|U|\\ge k+1\\), where \\(U\\) denotes the set of uncovered points.\n\n5. **How many uncovered points can sunny lines cover?**  \n\n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of the three special directions, it meets each row, each column and each diagonal in **at most one** point.  Consequently two distinct uncovered points that lie on the same sunny line must belong to different rows, different columns and different diagonals.  In particular a single sunny line can cover **at most one** of the points of the set \\(U\\) defined above (otherwise two points of \\(U\\) would share a row, a column or a diagonal, contradicting (2)).  \n\n   Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) uncovered points, while \\(|U|\\ge k+1\\).  This is impossible.\n\n   The contradiction shows that the assumption \\(k\\ge1\\) cannot hold; hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0 .\n   \\]\n\n6. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n\n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, none of them is sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Thus a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ for all }n\\ge 3}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you fin